## 6. Primitive vs Reference Types

```javascript
let user1 = "Priya";
let user2 = user1;

user1 = "Riya";

console.log(user1);
console.log(user2);

let favApps1 = ["Instagram", "Spotify"];
let favApps2 = favApps1;

favApps1.push("Zomato");

console.log(favApps1);
console.log(favApps2);

// Primitive values create a copy.
// Arrays are reference types, so both variables
// refer to the same array.
```

---

## 7. Primitive vs Object

```javascript
let favFood = "Pizza";
let favFoodCopy = favFood;

favFood = "Burger";

console.log(favFood);
console.log(favFoodCopy);

let zomatoUser = {
    city: "Vadodara"
};

let anotherUser = zomatoUser;

anotherUser.city = "Ahmedabad";

console.log(zomatoUser);
console.log(anotherUser);

// Primitive values are copied.
// Objects are stored by reference.
```

